{1600}{37c






Of the Frequency and History of Dread Armor
or
Scientific Ruminations on the Origins of Ancient Artifacts
Dr. William Wiggin-Bends







}
{1601}{37c


Dread Armor:  What is it?
}
{1602}{37l
It is not infrequently that a country farmer will be tilling his fields and unearth an old shield whose leather straps are rotted away, but whose metal still shines as the day it was forged.  Nor is it none other than commonplace that a city businessman might unearth a helmet with similar characteristics in the dirt of his basement's foundation.  These ancient pieces of armor, all bearing the same, strange insignia are found throughout Arcanum.  Over the years, due to the dark and insidious nature of their emblem, they have been given a name.

Dread Armor.

What do we know of this Dread Armor?  Historians from around the world have been researching this enigmatic phenomenon for years.  Logic would dictate that an intelligent man begin at the most distinctive feature-the emblem.  All Dread Armor bears the symbol of an eagle with a snake's head.  Extensive iconographical research has divulged little in the way of enlightenment concerning this talisman.  Various cultures and subcultures over the millennia have taken both the eagle and the snake as their representative symbols, but none in recorded history have taken them together.  Various rumors among circles less interested in science and more with the childishly fantastic have attributed this symbol to what is most often called the Derian-Ka, or ancient Order of the Dead.  Such claims are little more than wild speculation, as such a group has never been proven to exist, much less to forge their own armors.
}
{1603}{37l
Various experiments upon the metal itself have shown that it has enhanced magickal properties.  Experts in the field attribute the flows to Dark Necromancy, as well as minute traces of the Force and Elemental Colleges.  The exact age of the armor is unknown, but modern techniques have placed it near the Age of Legends.  Dread Armor has been found in almost every part of Arcanum, from the upper reaches of the Stonewall Mountains to the Dark Fens.  The honorable Quentin Payne, renowned explorer from the century last, purportedly found a full set of Dread Armor on one of his forays into the Vendigroth Wastes.
}
{1604}{37l
Perhaps in the future, history will be so kind as to shed some light on this most peculiar mystery.  Until that time, we can only guess at the reasons behind the mystery of Dread Armor.
}

{1610}{37c






Geographical and Magickal Properties of the Isle of Despair:
A Brief History of the Dreaded Penal Colony
by
Sir Isaac Thrush, esquire.







}
{1611}{37c


The Isle of Despair
An Overview
}
{1612}{37l
It has long been debated as to who first discovered the peculiar properties of the Isle of Despair, and how it came to be used as a prison colony.  There are historians who attribute this discovery the first master mages of Tulla, others who believe the elves found it during their journeys before their eventual building of Qintarra.  In any case, it is well-documented that the Isle of Despair has housed Arcanum's more diabolical social element for well over 400 years, and that it is a perfect environment for that element due to both its geographical and magickal properties.
}
{1613}{37l
Geographically, the Isle of Despair is perfect for housing criminals and derelicts.  Being that it is surrounded by water, with the nearest coast almost 1000 miles to the east, there is little danger of its inhabitants swimming to the mainland.  The Isle itself is situated between three strong ocean sub-currents; consequently, the waters around the island are invariably rough, and violently crash upon the rocky shore both day and night.  Massive whirlpools are known to appear suddenly and without warning.  There is an account of three Ashburian merchant ships being swallowed by one of these very whirlpools in 1783.
}
{1614}{37l
But it is not only the geographical characteristics of the island that make it the perfect prison.  Inexplicably, the island seems to negate certain types of magickal practices, but most importantly those of the Conveyance College.  Mages find it impossible to transport themselves from the island's surface to the mainland.  Various theories have been postulated concerning this phenomenon.  Some scientists believe that the island itself sits upon a massive vein of mithril ore.  Mithril, having the ability to harbor magickal flows, might just be the cause behind this most curious distortion.  Archaeologists have theorized that the Isle of Despair might once have harbored an ancient civilization that used an early form of technology, and that old ruins might exist beneath the island's surface to verify that fact.  Both theories might be scientifically sound, but none has yet been proven.

Regardless, the Isle of Despair will continue to harbor our social convicts until the day comes when its vagaries are no longer sufficient, or until we, as a society, no longer have the need for it.
}

{1620}{497C
Diary of Adam Quartermane
}

{1621}{497l
June 2, 1884. Finally ! I'm free of that blasted boarding school and all those stuffy old rules. I think I'll take a nice long respite before I apply myself to the dreary task of increasing our family fortune. 
Perhaps I should travel a bit. Maybe even spend a bit of time at our estate in Monaco - which, now that I think about it, many of the girls I had met in my teens should be grown by now. I think it's about time for some of them to become women.

July 4, 1884. I've finally made it to our estate in Monaco. The trip here was intermidable, the service aboard ship was atrocious, the weather was either blisteringly hot or pouring rain. Adding to that was the difficulty of getting the necessary funds from Papa. I'll swear he's the most miserly man who ever lived. What's a few thousand to someone worth millions ? 
At any rate, I've survived the trip - and strangely - Now that I think about it, I actually kind of enjoyed the traveling. Maybe I inherited my grandfather's adventurous spirit. 
In a few days the servants will have the mansion cleaned and livable, then let the girls beware. Adam Quartermane is in town!}

{1622}{37l(Wine and other stains has rendered numerous entries virtually unreadable but scattered near-pornographic sentences can be made out)
}

{1623}{497l October 29, 1884. I've been shot ! The wound is but a nasty gouge on my left arm but it hurts fearfully. Annabelle's father had caught us together in a most compromising situation in the Maiden's Walk Gardens. I had thought she was ripe for the taking. I seem to have been in error as she protested my advances with distressing volume.
Fortunately, Sir Tarellond-Ashe didn't wish to hit his beloved daughter (I wonder if she still is ?) and thus, I was able to escape with only this nasty wound, and a few thousand scratches from my flight through the roses, bushes, and hedges.
Now that I home and safe - it's odd - but I find myself looking back at this evening with considerable enjoyment. I think I actually enjoyed the danger I was in. 
For a while though, I think it would be best I remained close to home. After all, even my grandfather knew when to take time off and relax.

November 7, 1885. I've come across a rather ancient manuscript in our mansion's attic. Although it is naught but a collection of myths and fables from the Age of Darkness, one story did catch my eye.
It tells of a long-forgotten place called the Tomb of Acererak. Bloody tongue-twister of a name! But it inspired such a dream of exploration that I found myself relishing the very thought of seeing places and things that no human has beheld for over a thousand years.
And let's not forget about the treasure hoard suggested by the tale. If such a treasure does exist, then I'll have adaquate funds to last me a lifetime. Or at least enough for further adventures.
As for the deadly demi-lich and his ilk, I'll simply hire enough trigger-happy goons to blast them to a million pieces! After all, a demilich is a creature of magic and magic is helpless when confronted by superior technology. 
Who wants to be a stuffy old banker anyway? Not me, I realize that now. I would like to hear my name mentioned in the same breath as Franklin Payne; before his, of course.
As soon as I'm well enough}
{1624}{497l to travel, I believe I shall return home and raise some money for this little adventure.

I've copied the story here so that when historians tell of my exploits they will know what inspired me to become such a famous explorer.
}
{1625}{497c
Legend of Acererak's Tomb
}
{1626}{497l Somewhere under a lost and lonely hill of grim and foreboding aspect lies a labyrinthine crypt filled with terrible traps and not a few strong and ferocious monsters to slay the unwary. 
A survivor of the Slaughter of Kree, Acererak was already a powerful wizard when he came upon an ancient knowledge that ultimately tranformed him into a demilich. Able to destroy the very souls of those he slew, Acererak became a terrifying danger to all the southlands.
A mighty force of arms called the Darian Ka rose up and chased the foul creature to the far side of the Grey Mountains and ultimately trapped him within a tomb in Diebolde Valley. A monastary was constructed in front of the entrance to disuade the casual graverobber from breaking the seals but it would have long since fallen into ruin.
It is rumoured that rich treasures both precious and magical fill the crypt, but in addition to the aforementioned guardians, some say a demilich still wards his final haunt. (Be warned that tales told have it that this being possesses powers that make him nearly undefeatable!) 
Accounts relate that adventurers have an extremely low probability of ever finding the chamber where the demilich Acererak lingers, for the passages and rooms of the tomb are fraught with terrible traps, poison gases, and magical protections. Furthermore, the demilich has hidden his lair so well, that even those who avoid the pitfalls likely will not locate their true goal. 
So only large and well-prepared parties of the bravest and strongest should even consider the attempt, and if they do locate the tomb, they must be prepared to fail.
Any expedition must be composed of characters of high level and varied class. They must have magical protections and weapons, and they should equip themselves with every sort of device possible to ensure their survival.
}

{1627}{37l(Numerous pages have been ripped out and only parts of the last two entries remains)
}
{1628}{497l On another note: T'Rhelliou has her linens in a knot again about sensing orcs. I'll swear that elf-witch sees orcs hiding behind every bush.
Just to make her shut up for a while I've ordered our tents set up in the monastary ruins. The remanents of the walls will protect her should those superstitious animals ever get brave enough to come within a hundred leagues of....



.... we soon made short work of the wretched things within! Acererak - I'm sure it was him - died in a blizzard of bullets at the base of his unholy throne.
We had to retreat though, as the foul air quickly had us all gagging. In the morning we'll begin a proper exploration of the tomb. 
Rawlun just informed me that we're down to a total of two hundred bullets. I'm not worried. 
The demilich is dead, and there's nothing that dangerous left anywh....
}
