{1710}{37l 
Forgotten Places 1.0
May 2006 to June 2007

How did I start this?....

Reprinted from Terra-Arcanum
August 20, 2007:

Long story short, I found a piece of software included in the game called Arcanum World Editor. I started it up, then about went bat-shit because there was no instruction book and none of the arrow keys would allow for scrolling. On top of that, no matter what I created, I couldn't seem to get back to it after a shutdown. Eventually I gave up on it,
Found the Dimensions of Arcanum website a week or so later and became a lurking regular trying to learn anything I could about how to tweak the game.
Then one day I heard about something called Car-Arcanum, and imagining my party all piling into a Stanley Steamer to head out, I downloaded the thing. Didn't get the Steamer I'd imagined, but I did notice a very modified game. Bingo! An old fantasy about adding new adventures to a video game roared back to like. I needed to learn how to do this.
About this time, the Dimensions of Arcanum website started acting up so I looked around a bit more and stumbled across this place.
After a couple months of being a lurker reading all the old posts, I made about 6 - maybe 7 attempts at a small mod. Those turned out soooo baaadd.... I just deleted them as a matter of course. 
I realized I'd probably do better by modifying any shortcomings I saw in the stock game but was still unclear as to how to get into it. Between needing that info and getting at the download section here, I joined.
After getting the answers I needed for how to unpack and (eventually) what a patch file really was, I unpacked Car-Arcanum completely, began studying it, and working my way through AMTUT at the same time. Eventually, it started making sense and I felt ready to try.
The ground floor of the Shrouded Hills temple was my first effort. Other than the bookcases and the door for downstairs, what you see is what I built. After TONGSyaBASS}{1711}{37l was good enough to enlighten me as to what a patch file really was, and I was finally able to see my creation in an existing video game, there was no turning back.

After the temple interior came other things like adding a forest and small village to the Ruby Glade, then back to Shrouded Hills for a cemetary, then Mrs. Owens - now you know why the Constable never goes home! - and adding the entry to the temple to replace the dirt.
Then I got this brilliant idea of adding a new mystery quest which required me to build an entire small island. 
That turned out to be clunky and unworkable so I killed the links that would have taken you there and focused on other challenges, including adding the various hacks. (Many of which didn't actually work)
After dumping the hacks into place and seeing that they appearently worked, I turned my attention back to game enhancement for a while.
My idea of enhancement included such things as expanding the hell of of the Uncharted Cave and, even more so, The Iron Clan map. That required a completely new map to hold the massive creation.
Feeling rather cocky about my success with the Iron Clan remodel, I went to work on an idea about adding a rescue quest in a new section of the Void.

Reprinted from Terra-Arcanum
April 02, 2007:

The story goes that when Arronax had his hissey fit and cast the spell that destroyed Vendigroth, the most powerful magic spell ever cast slammed head-first into the greatest concentration of advanced technology the world had ever seen and something had to give.
A bubble universe was created that exists outside space and time with regards to Arcanum. All the people and animals who were caught in that bubble mutated into reptilian lifeforms which adapted to the new enviroment, lived, bred, and died for many generations.
Using the remaining technology there were two escape attempts made; one was successful but the second attemt failed because the town was overrun by Void Lizards and Araya. That was }
{1712}{37lwhat you know as Liam Cameron's Portal.
Now, getting to the Void; As a rule, the only way for an Arcanumite to enter the Void is through the Banishment spell - however... At the bottom edge of the bubble was the port city of Vorakia, and for reasons unknown, the two worlds meet tight there. For those who are both barave enough and strong enough, it is possible to cross over into the universe of the Void at that one spot and meet the last decendants of the people who had been tranported into this other reality.

Now building Vorakia wasn't too difficult, adding a few ruined places along the coast to entice the player along was easy, and even creating an interesting underground to explore wasn't too bad, but then the problems began.
The area I'd created for Vorakia was entirely way too large. Now luckily, I had built the place in such a way that I was able to split the city into two by adding a salt marsh with an iron bridge connecting them. The newer-looking section was beefed up with technology and named Adia.
From Vorakia I made a portal where you pop up into a section of tunnel that serves as a doorway into the world of the Void. 
Problem solved, right? Wrong. 
The new Void I began building needed a huge area to play in and that required a way for players to get around in a reasonable amount of time.  
I abandoned the new Void map and began building the new Void onto the main map and even modified the world map to allow for movement. 
Great idea! Except....
I had finally decided that one day I would release this work for others to play with and that set up one hell of a problem. The only ways to get this new world map to the players would be to send the entire Arcanum.dat (four gigabytes!) or a picture of the changed area that could be printed out. Neither of which sounded good.
Fate stepped in very shortly afterwards and postponed the decision on what to do about the map as a major glitch erradicated everything I had built in the new Void.
}
