{1841}{37l
  Over the shrill noises from above her, Marie caught the sound of something falling through the foliage. A glance up revealed an off-white oval object dropping more or less straight towards her. Without thinking, she cupped her hands and caught the heavy egg. Relief flashed through her as this was an egg rather than a mass of feces, but that quickly vanished as the shell broke into several pieces upon impact.
  Dismayed shock became wonder as the baby contained within the egg began to move. Through the gaps formed by the breaks in the four-inch long egg, the chick could be seen. Greenish gray it was and wrinkled in odd places. As Marie watched silently, the chick straightened its neck, moving the large portion of shell that still covered it head. Thick, viscous fluid poured from the shattered egg as the membrane ruptured, coating the palms of Marie’s hands. 
  Marie ignored the slimy, warm fluids; she was more interested the new life that was now cradled in her hands. Feel soon told her that the tiny creature had survived its traumatic hatching unharmed but there was something truly odd but the sensations as well. The chick had too many limbs - six to be precise. Was it a freak? 
  Whatever the case, in spite of her upbringing, she made up her mind that the youngster had earned the right to have at least a few minutes of life. Carefully moving the broken egg onto her left hand, she began picking away the fragments, freeing the chick from its ruined enclosure.
  Helplessly grinning at the small miracle in her hand, she finished by peeling back the tough membrane and sliding the bottom of the shell from under the newly born chick.
  The six limbs were no genetic accident. In her hand there now lay an honest-to-God dragon.}
{1842}{37l
  It was lying on its side, giving her a good look at it profile. A semi-triangular head was attached to a short muscular neck, which joined to a barrel-chested torso crowded with appendages. The eyes were still closed but appeared to be set far enough forward to allow for stereoscopic vision. A short snout held two slits for the nostrils and a bump on the end that could only be for breaking free from the shell. The jaws were presently slack and Marie thought she could see tiny nubs of teeth partially hidden behind thin, fleshy lips. 
  The dragonet had a reptilian appearance but its heartbeat, which Marie could feel, was entirely too fast for it to be a lizard. This creature had either a bird’s or mammal’s metabolism, not a reptile’s. Smooth textured skin covered the small form,  its wrinkles would soon disappear.
  Its limbs were folded against its body. Its wings were even bent over the back. At the end of each foot, she counted three toes facing forward and two spur-like toes on the back. Each of the toes was already equipped with claws, which would still be extremely soft but would soon harden. This would be a creature that, if the spur claws were functional, would have an inescapable grasp.
  Marie breathed a quiet laugh at the wrinkled little thing as it slowly stretched its four legs and began unfolding the under-formed bat-like wings that projected from its shoulders. These wings were membranes that would run from the struts at the shoulders to where they joined the torso at the hips. She noted it also had a tail that was currently curled up and rested against its stomach.
  It had yet to take its first breath or even to open its eyes, but continued its stretching movements and added the uncurling of its short tail to its tasks. In moments, the vestigial remains of the yoke-sack was visible on the grayish belly. }
{1843}{37l
  With an obvious effort, the tail finally slipped free from between the hind legs, giving Marie her first glimpse of the dragon’s length. Once it had fully extended, the baby dragon was just over six inches long from nose to tail – and one of the most beautiful things she had ever seen.
  Marie used a finger to wipe away the mucus that covered the baby’s mouth. 
  “Com’on baby, breathe,” she whispered. “Breathe.”
  As if it had been waiting for that moment, the dragonet raised its head, opened its jaws and took its first breath. With a small, keening cry, the little fellow announced his arrival into the world.
  "Saaarrrreeeeeooossssssssss," it called. Then it lay back, exhausted by the effort. 
  “Sareos,” Marie muttered with another smile. “Sareos… A good name for you.”
  The little dragon soon opened his eyes and blearily gazed out into the world. Shaking from the effort, it pivoted its head and beheld its benefactor.
  With a mild shock Marie Felt its contentment as it lay it head back down. These creatures impress, she realized, just like many species of birds. Sareos had assumed that Marie was his mother. 
